## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design automated degradation with controlled risk during external dependency pressure and multi-region operation.

## Key Concepts
- Circuit breakers and failover
- Per-region degradation strategies
- Idempotent queues and exactly-once delivery
- Canary and synthetic testing
- Rollback criteria and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for circuit breaker state machine
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(threshold, resetTime) {
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.resetTime = resetTime;
    this.failures = 0;
    this.state = 'CLOSED';
  }
  onFailure() {
    this.failures++;
    if (this.failures > this.threshold) this.state = 'OPEN';
  }
  canRequest() { return this.state !== 'OPEN'; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for multi-tenant services with mixed SLIs?
- How would you validate degradation paths in canaries without impacting customers?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to handle multi-region routing faults and basic SRE tooling without outsourcing to advanced chaos scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Regional observability: latency, error rate, saturation per region
- Alerting: region-specific thresholds
- Traffic control: region-aware routing and small canaries
- Rollback: quick gateway reversion and regional validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight region-aware retry example (conceptual)
async function routeRequest(regionMap, path){
  const region = selectHealthyRegion(regionMap);
  const res = await fetch(`https://${region}${path}`);
  if(!res.ok) throw new Error('Region failed');
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate that the incident is resolved after rollback?
- What non-functional tests would you run before promoting a region back to full traffic?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design scalable, tenant-aware incident response and degradation at scale. It tests how candidates balance automated safeguards with precise human oversight and how runbooks integrate metrics, backfills, and recovery verification.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-region, multi-tenant SLAs
- Degradation strategies and traffic shaping
- Cache strategy and data consistency during outages
- Canary testing, rollback criteria, post-incident validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo per-tenant degradation switch
function route(request){
  const t = request.tenant;
  if (t.degradation === 'high') return routeToCacheOnly();
  return routeToFullPath();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate this runbook in a synthetic outage? 
- Which dashboards and alerting thresholds would you use for tenants at risk?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to respond to production incidents with a concrete, beginner-friendly plan focusing on observable signals, containment, rollback, and validation under time pressure.

## Key Concepts
- Observability: latency p95, error rate, queue depth, redis/backlog metrics
- Containment: throttle concurrency, pause new work, revert patch safely
- Rollback & Validation: restore previous config, synthetic tests, end-to-end checks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple end-to-end validation stub
async function checkDelivery(queueClient, producer) {
  const msg = {id: 'test', payload: 'ping'};
  await producer.send(msg);
  const ok = await queueClient.awaitDeliveryAck(msg.id, 5000);
  return ok;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate the rollback trigger and rollback-on-fail thresholds?
- Which metrics would you add to distinguish transient spikes from persistent issues?
